course: dictionary
lesson: 11043
title: Many meanings of one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer means. The word weave is one of them. «The town of Baarle has a border that weaves through streets and even houses.» Here weave means to move in and out, changing direction, not to make cloth.
explain: When weave means making cloth, it means crossing threads, strips or twigs over and under each other. «Male weaver birds weave hanging nests from grass strips.» The birds are not making cloth, but they are crossing strips over and under each other to build a nest, so the meaning is still about crossing and weaving.
explain: To weave a story means to make it up by joining many parts together. «She wove a tale about a brave dragon.» Nobody is making cloth or moving between things here. Try each meaning in the sentence and see which one still makes sense.
fact: Weave is a verb, and its past tense is wove (or woven for the past participle).
fact: To weave means to make cloth, a basket or a mat by crossing threads, strips or twigs over and under each other.
fact: To weave can also mean to move in and out between things, changing direction. The past tense for this meaning is weaved.
fact: To weave a story means to make it up, especially by joining many parts.
fact: A weave is the pattern or way that something is woven, as in «a tight weave».
exercise: «Male weaver birds weave hanging nests from grass strips, tying knots with beak and feet.» What does weave mean here? | *To crossing strips over and under each other, which is making a nest | To make a loud noise | To swim quickly | To make something by crossing strips over and under each other
exercise: In safety lessons we hear, «Cyclists should not weave between cars.» What does weave mean here? | *To move in and out between things, changing direction | To tell a made-up story | To make cloth from threads | To shout a warning
exercise: What does weave mean in «The cloth has a tight weave»? | A tight knot | A fast run | *The pattern or way the threads are woven | A loud song
test: «Real spiders cannot write words, but some weave zigzag silk decorations into their webs.» What does weave mean here? | To run away fast | To cook in a pot | To sing softly | *To make by crossing silk threads
test: Which sentence uses weave to mean move in and out, changing direction? | «The harvest mouse is Britain's smallest rodent.» | *«The border between Belgium and the Netherlands weaves through streets and even houses.» | «Birds build many kinds of nests, including woven baskets.» | «Real spiders cannot write words.»
exam: «She wove a tale about a brave dragon.» What does weave mean here? | *To make up a story by joining many parts | To hide a secret | To cook a meal | To cut paper into strips
